                                   ==========
                                   metasearch
                                   ==========

                      https://github.com/mat-1/metasearch2

----
INFO
----

metasearch (aka metasearch2) is a cute metasearch engine. It sources its results
from Google, Bing, Brave, and several others. It's designed to be as lightweight
as possible, both on the server and client. There is no required client-side
JavaScript.

There's a public demo instance at https://s.matdoes.dev, but please do not use
it as your default or rely on it. This is so I don't get ratelimited by Google
or other engines. Also, searches are logged. Run your own instance instead!

------------
INSTALLATION
------------

The easiest way to install metasearch is with `cargo install metasearch`. To get
the unstable version with the latest features, you can install it with
`cargo install --git https://github.com/mat-1/metasearch2`.

Usage: `metasearch [config_file]`

The config_file argument is optional; if it's not specified then it'll be
checked at the following locations:

  - $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/metasearch/config.toml
  - $HOME/.config/metasearch/config.toml
  - ./config.toml

If no config file exists, it'll be created at the first valid path in the list.

By default, metasearch runs on port 28019. You are encouraged to use a reverse
proxy.

-------------
CONFIGURATION
-------------

You can see all the default config options at `src/config.rs`. Some interesting
options you may want to change are:

  - bind - the host and port that the web server runs on, defaults to
    `0.0.0.0:28019`.
  - api - whether your instance is accessible through a JSON API. See below for
    more details.
  - ui.stylesheet_url - a link to a stylesheet that will be loaded alongside the
    main one, for example `/themes/catppuccin-mocha.css`.
  - image_search.enabled - add a tab for viewing image results for your query.
    this is disabled by default as the image proxy could be used to make GET
    requests to arbitrary URLs from your server.
  - engines.google.weight - the ranking score multiplier for an engine, you can
    modify this if you prefer the results from certain engines.
  - safe_search - `off`, `moderate` or `strict` (also on the settings page).
    Explicit results are filtered through engine safe-search parameters and a
    local blocklist that also covers the local index, image results and the
    image proxy.

--------
OFFLINE
--------

metasearch can keep serving searches when the machine it runs on has no
internet access:

  - Successful searches are cached in the local sqlite database and can be
    served again without contacting any engine. Fresh entries are served
    immediately; expired entries are served when every engine that needs the
    network fails. Answers that depend on the requester (ip, user agent) or
    the current time are never cached. The cache is enabled by default and can
    be configured or disabled under `[cache]`. On systemd/container setups set
    `[database] path` to a persistent path so the cache and index survive.
  - ZIM archives (offline Wikipedia and friends) can be searched too. The
    container image bundles kiwix-serve; it runs on loopback and is
    reverse-proxied at /kiwix, so one container on one port serves everything.
    Put ZIM files in the `./zim` directory (subdirectories are fine), then:

        docker compose up --build

    The directory is scanned at container startup. To add a ZIM without
    restarting the container:

        docker exec <container> kiwix-manage /cache/kiwix-library.xml add /zim/new.zim

    (the library is rebuilt from `./zim` on the next start). A ZIM needs a
    fulltext index to be searchable; most wikipedia mini/maxi archives have
    one.

Making your own ZIM from a website with [zimit](https://github.com/openzim/zimit):

    mkdir -p zim
    docker run --rm -v "$PWD/zim:/output" ghcr.io/openzim/zimit \
      zimit --seeds https://example.com --name example
    docker compose restart metasearch2

Prebuilt corpora are available at https://library.kiwix.org. zimit is not part
of the metasearch image because it needs a headless browser.

--------------------------
SITE RULES AND LOCAL INDEX
--------------------------

metasearch keeps a local sqlite database (`[database]`, by default
`$XDG_CACHE_HOME/metasearch/metasearch.db`) with three things:

  - The search response cache (see the offline section above).
  - Site rules: raise, lower or hide a domain from the "ranking" menu under
    each search result. Rules are subdomain-aware score multipliers, listed
    on the settings page, where individual rules can be undone (that also
    un-hides a domain) or cleared. Theme, custom CSS and safe search live there
    too.
  - The personal index: every result of a search is recorded and can be
    searched again later through the `index` engine, even when the network is
    down. `[database] max_results` and `max_age_days` bound the index; the
    oldest and least recently seen results are pruned automatically.

The rules and index are stored per instance, not per browser.

The database runs in WAL mode with immediate write transactions. At startup it
runs `PRAGMA quick_check`: a corrupt file is moved aside as
`metasearch.db.corrupt-*` and the instance starts fresh instead of refusing to
start. Pending schema migrations back up the database to
`metasearch.db.pre-migration.bak` first, and `synchronous = "full"` (default)
fsyncs every commit. Keep the database on local disk; sqlite locking is not
reliable on some network filesystems.

--------
JSON API
--------

metasearch has a JSON API that can be enabled by setting `api = true` in your
config. To use it, set the `Accept: application/json` header in your requests.

For example:
curl 'http://localhost:28019/search?q=sandcats' -H 'Accept: application/json'

The structure of the API is not guaranteed to be stable, as it relies on
serializing internal structs. It may change without warning in the future.
